<p>If you operate a commercial vessel in Canada, two things determine whether your season runs smoothly or grinds to a halt: passing your Transport Canada wharf audits without costly downtime, and ensuring your crew has the right equipment if something goes sideways in the North Atlantic.</p>
<p>Commercial vessels operating in Canadian waters must comply with safety requirements regulated under the Canada Shipping Act, 2001. This article breaks down the practical commercial vessel safety equipment requirements that apply to your specific hull, your crew, and your paperwork, so you can operate with confidence every time an inspector steps aboard your boat.</p>

<h2>What Vehicles Are Classified as Commercial?</h2>
<p>Under the Canada Shipping Act, 2001, if a vessel is used for any business, work, or payment, it is legally a commercial vessel. Transport Canada mandates adherence to strict standards for small commercial vessels, and the rules apply regardless of how recreational the hull might look.</p>
<p>The specific regional operations that must carry commercial-grade gear include:</p>
<ul>
    <li>Whale-watching RIBs operating out of Digby, NS.</li>
    <li>Paid fishing charters operating out of Bonavista, NL.</li>
    <li>Tour vessels and water taxis operating out of Charlottetown, PEI.</li>
    <li>Workboats and crew-transfer launches for aquaculture sites.</li>
</ul>
<p>Commercial fishing vessels fall primarily under the Fishing Vessel Safety Regulations (FVSR), but the core gear requirements remain strict for any hull earning revenue on Atlantic waters.</p>

<h3>Compliance vs. Crew Safety Obligations</h3>
<p>A captain's responsibility covers two separate areas that inspectors evaluate independently:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>The Vessel:</strong> Hull registration, construction requirements, stability documentation, and life raft capacities. Commercial vessels must maintain proper damage stability and watertight integrity to prevent flooding. Owners must maintain their vessels in good working condition.</li>
    <li><strong>The Crew:</strong> Providing mandatory personal protective gear, including lifejackets, immersion suits, and safety training, for every person on board, including deckhands, guides, and staff.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Tracking Transport Canada Updates</h3>
<p>Regulations shift. Equipment gets recalled. The way to stay current is through Ship Safety Bulletins (SSBs), the official channel Transport Canada uses to announce amended rules, equipment recalls, and safety alerts. The recent SSB-09/2026, for example, reinforced maintenance and labelling requirements for life-saving appliances under both SVR and FVSR.</p>
<p>Fleet managers should monitor these bulletins or contact the regional Transport Canada Marine Safety Office in Dartmouth, NS, to confirm their specific gear list before inspection season. Operators must also assess risks and report marine accidents without delay as part of continuous safety improvement.</p>

<h2>The Core Rulebooks (SVR vs. FVSR)</h2>
