<p>The Small Commercial Vessel Safety Guide (TP 14070) outlines safety requirements for smaller vessels under SVR. Vessel owners must ensure compliance with the Canada Shipping Act regardless of which rulebook governs their specific hull. Internationally, the International Maritime Organization develops key requirements with treaties like SOLAS and MARPOL, while SOLAS dictates minimum standards for construction, equipment, and operation of commercial vessels. The ISM Code requires the implementation of a Safety Management System for many vessels, and the ISPS Code addresses maritime security requirements for ships engaged on international voyages. Compliance with the Marine Safety Management System Regulations is also mandatory in British Columbia for applicable operations.</p>
<p>Failing to know which rulebook applies to your specific vessel size and tonnage is the fastest way to fail an initial wharf audit.</p>

<h2>Audit Checklist: Commercial Vessel Safety Documentation</h2>
<p>Under Canadian law, every commercial vessel must designate an authorized representative-typically the owner or fleet manager-who is legally responsible for the boat's daily compliance and crew training.</p>
<p>Transport Canada inspectors will ask for your paperwork before they look at your gear. Operators should maintain a dedicated onboard binder containing:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Commercial Registration:</strong> Proof the vessel is entered in the Small Vessel Register. A pleasure craft licence is completely invalid for commercial work.</li>
