</ul>

<h2>Construction Standards & Vessel Modifications with Safety Integrity</h2>
<p>Small commercial vessels must meet the Construction Standards for Small Vessels (TP 1332) that were active when the boat was built, imported, or converted to commercial use. This covers hull integrity, fuel systems, ventilation, and engine compartment protection. If your vessel was built after April 2011, it must feature a permanently attached compliance notice stating its maximum passenger capacity, propulsion limits, and design restrictions.</p>
<p><strong>The Modification Trap:</strong> Repurposing a retired Atlantic fishing hull for tourism, or adding heavy deck gear, changes the boat's centre of gravity. Because owners are legally responsible for ensuring adequate stability, major structural modifications alter your stability criteria. You must notify Transport Canada and potentially upgrade the vessel to meet modern construction standards. Even changes to metal fittings, rope handling systems, or engine placement can trigger a reassessment.</p>

<h2>Commercial Vessel Safety Equipment: Life-Saving Appliances</h2>
<p>Transport Canada divides safety gear into two distinct categories that inspectors check at the wharf:</p>
<p><strong>Life-Saving Appliances</strong> - personal equipment meant to keep crew alive in the water:</p>
<ul>
    <li>Transport Canada approved lifejackets for every person on board. Lifejackets must fit each person individually.</li>
    <li>Lifebuoys at least 600 mm in diameter.</li>
    <li>Buoyant heaving lines with a minimum 15 m of rope.</li>
    <li>Reboarding ladders where freeboard is significant.</li>
    <li>Vessels over 8.5 meters must carry at least one liferaft; inflatable liferafts must be serviced regularly.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Vessel Safety Equipment</strong> - shipboard gear to keep the hull afloat:</p>
<ul>
    <li>Manual bilge pump, required for vessels over 6 meters in length. Automatic bilge pumps are recommended as backup.</li>
    <li>Anchors and sufficient rode.</li>
    <li>Navigation lights displayed per Collision Regulations.</li>
    <li>Sound signals, such as an electric horn or whistle, depending on vessel length.</li>
    <li>A watertight flashlight is required on all small commercial vessels.</li>
    <li>Every small commercial vessel must have a first aid kit on board.</li>
</ul>
<p>Emergency equipment must include life rafts and approved personal flotation devices. Vessels must be equipped with approved life-saving appliances, fire safety equipment, and distress alerting gear. Crew must be trained to use life-saving equipment effectively-gear cannot just sit on a checklist. Spartan Marine stocks these exact approved items across their Atlantic branches, ensuring operators can rent or purchase compliant equipment without delay.</p>

<h2>Fire Suppression Requirements for Commercial Vessels</h2>
<p>Any motorized commercial hull with fuel-burning engines or enclosed cabins must carry approved portable fire extinguisher units rated for marine use.</p>
<p>Inspectors look for specific 5BC or 10BC rated units mounted in plain sight near exits and machinery spaces. Every extinguisher must feature an up-to-date, legible inspection tag. Key details to note:</p>
<ul>
    <li>At least one extinguisher near the engine compartment and one near the helm or exit.</li>
    <li>Extinguishers must be fully charged and accessible, not buried under gear in limited storage.</li>
    <li>Larger vessels or those with enclosed passenger spaces may require fixed fire suppression systems and dedicated smoke or heat detectors in accommodation areas.</li>
</ul>
<p>Regular onboard drills should include locating and operating each fire extinguisher and simulating an engine-room fire scenario.</p>

<h2>Distress Alerting and Emergency Signalling Equipment</h2>
<p>Navigating fog-prone Atlantic waters with enough water beneath the keel to stay safe requires reliable communication and visibility tools. COLREGs defines rules of the road for navigation and collision avoidance, and every operator must know them.</p>
<p>To pass a random Transport Canada wharf inspection, a 7-9 metre vessel must carry:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>VHF Marine Radio:</strong> The primary communication tool for Coast Guard-assisted rescue. Radio operators must hold the appropriate certificate.</li>
    <li><strong>Visual Distress Signals:</strong> Approved pyrotechnic marine flares, verified to be within their legal expiration window. Flares must be approved by Transport Canada and expire after four years from the date of manufacture.</li>
    <li><strong>Navigation Hardware:</strong> Magnetic compass, sound signalling devices, and radar reflectors for non-metal hulls. Navigational equipment must be appropriate and properly maintained for commercial voyages.</li>
</ul>
<p>Operators on a near coastal class voyage or working at distance from shore should consult Transport Canada guidance on whether an EPIRB or other distress beacon is required for their specific voyage category.</p>

<h2>Crew Training and Marine Safety Competency</h2>
<p>A fully equipped boat is only safe if the crew is certified. Transport Canada requires operators and deckhands to hold valid Marine Personnel Regulations credentials based on their specific vessel class:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Small Vessel Operator Proficiency (SVOP):</strong> Mandatory for operators of small commercial vessels up to 5 gross tonnage for passenger vessels or 15 gross tonnage for workboats.</li>
    <li><strong>Marine Emergency Duties (MED / SDV-BS):</strong> Crucial safety training for crew members covering firefighting, vessel abandonment, and cold-water survival procedures.</li>
    <li><strong>Restricted Operator Certificate (ROC-M):</strong> Mandatory for anyone operating a VHF marine radio set on a commercial vessel.</li>
</ul>
<p>Captains must deliver pre-departure safety briefings to passengers, including PFD demonstrations, and conduct documented emergency drills for man overboard, fire, and flooding scenarios. In Atlantic Canada, this training must specifically address cold shock, hypothermia risk, and rapid weather changes, the local hazards that make complacency lethal.</p>

<h2>Maintaining Your Commercial Vessel Safety Equipment & Audit Readiness</h2>
<p>Compliance is not a one-time event. Transport Canada can board a vessel at any time to verify that your gear is in safe operating condition. Operators should use official small commercial vessel checklists to run routine self-audits across all gear before leaving the wharf.</p>
<p><strong>Record-Keeping Essentials:</strong></p>
<ul>
    <li>Mechanical maintenance logs for pumps, steering, and engine systems.</li>
    <li>Crew training records, drill logs, and certification renewals.</li>
    <li>Daily voyage plans tracking Environment Canada marine forecasts, tides, and fog risks.</li>
    <li>Equipment expiry logs tracking flares, life raft service tags, and extinguisher inspection dates.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>How Spartan Helps:</strong> Spartan Industrial Marine operates as the local compliance partner for Atlantic fleets, offering certified life raft servicing, asset tracking for equipment expiry dates, and a reliable supply chain of approved safety gear. Whether you need to replace expired flares or restock your vessel safety tools before the next audit, contact Spartan Marine to keep your fleet completely audit-ready.</p>
